// EXPECT: PASS
// README "Contextual Extension"/"Contextual Aliasing": a trailing-dot
// value assignment reached through an *inherited* field must extend the
// instance actually being navigated to, not the supertype that happens
// to declare the field. Adapted from README's full "Contextual Aliasing"
// worked example (Person/Surname/joe smith/New Context), with the Alias
// part removed - creating the alias on Person's own contextual extension
// isn't visible to joe smith's lookup at all yet (see cpp/ToDo, "Whether/
// how a contextual extension should apply through an Alias"), a separate,
// deeper, not-yet-fixed gap. This test isolates the other half: once
// Surname is found (by its real name, via ordinary inheritance from
// Person), does "New Context.joe smith.Surname. = 'Schmidt';" correctly
// extend joe smith - not Person?
//
// A real bug was found and fixed here: current_context() used to compute
// the extension target as the found field's own *declaring* object
// (Person, via handle.parent()) rather than the object this statement is
// actually working within (joe smith, via the new Frame::scope_parent) -
// so this assignment used to silently widen to a *global* extension of
// Person, visible in principle to every Person, not just joe smith.
Person: {
	Surname: String;
}
New Context: {
	joe smith: Person {
		Surname ~= 'Smith';
	}
}
New Context.joe smith.Surname.
	= 'Schmidt';
